          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"verona-colei-che-non-si-deve-amare-60501e05fb417875\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.96,\"analysis\":\"Rafa’s embedded “ti amerei” occurs under negation in the counterfactual construction “altrimenti non ti amerei” (“otherwise I would not love you”). In context, this reasoning presents his present love for Loretta as true and as grounded partly in profound respect. It neither undertakes a commitment nor functions as an affect-driven verbal discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"Loretta disputes Rafa’s characterization, arguing that he desires rather than loves her; this raises uncertainty about sincerity or self-understanding, not about the utterance’s strongly truth-conditional force.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Rafa directly addresses Loretta, embedding the first-person love expression in the conditional claim that without profound respect he would not love her.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Rafa uses the claim to reassure Loretta and rebut her suggestion that he considers her frivolous. Loretta immediately rejects his avowal and contrasts love with mere desire, citing his conduct in bringing her there. The exchange may involve courtship or persuasion, but the core expression still presents Rafa’s love as an existing fact rather than enacting a relational undertaking. Nothing indicates reflex-like or uncontrolled production.\" ,\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Per te sento anche un profondo rispetto: altrimenti non ti amerei.”\",\"supports\":\"The explicit feeling report and counterfactual consequent pragmatically present Rafa as currently loving Loretta, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Loretta replies: “voi mi desiderate... Ma quanto ad amarmi, è ben altra cosa.”\",\"supports\":\"Her response treats Rafa’s words as a disputable claim about his emotional state, reinforcing truth-conditional analysis while questioning its truth or sincerity.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Rafa is rebutting Loretta’s claim that he considers her frivolous and says he also feels profound respect for her.\",\"supports\":\"The utterance serves as reasoned reassurance rather than a commitment or expressive/reflexive discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is marked as affirmative modal/conditional, with the exact match “ti amerei.”\",\"supports\":\"Confirms that the love expression is grammatically conditional, while its enclosing “otherwise not” construction supports a present avowal in context.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core force as an avowal presented through counterfactual reasoning. Reassurance, possible persuasion, and Loretta’s disbelief are contextual features and create no residual pressure beyond T/P/E.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The grammatical conditional does not make the event hypothetical in utterance-status terms: it is a direct conversational avowal expressed through an embedded counterfactual.\"}"
        }
      ],
